'''Estuarian Creole''' or '''''Créyòl''''', also known as ''Volisenya Marjalenca'' ({{Literal translation|marshland Volisanian}}) is a [[Volisanian-based creole languages|Volisanian-based creole]] language spoken by more than ten million people, mostly in the [[Estuaria|Estuarian community]], within the [[Baliscan Confederation]]. It is one of two official languages of Estuaria, alongside [[Volisanian language|Volisanian]].

The language emerged from contact between Volisan settlers, indigenous peoples, and enslaved Kaftians during the [[Kaftian slave trade]] in the [[Governorate of Sant Vicenç|Volisan colony of Sant Vicenç]]. Although its vocabulary is mostly taken from phonetic 18th-century Volisanian, its grammar and sentence structure has some properties of that of a Central Kaftian language, particularly the [[W:Yoruba language|Imo language]]. It also has influences from Xacano, Bayani, Beron, and other [[Languages of Kaftia|Kaftian languages]].

Because of the extreme similarities between Volisanian and [[Beron language|Beron]], it is difficult to distinguish the exact origin of each word. Though there are different theories about its origins, most linguists now believe that ''Créyòl'' originated from the Catalan dialects of [[W:Valencian Community|Valencia]].

